@Server_HostAddress = http://localhost:5476

# Keycloak, as the AppHost exposes it. The port is assigned per run — take it from the Aspire
# dashboard's `keycloak` resource, NOT from `docker ps`: those are different ports, because Aspire
# fronts the container with a proxy. Keycloak derives a token's `iss` from the address it was called
# on, so a token minted against the published container port carries an issuer the API has never
# heard of and comes back 401. The realms and clients are in FieldKit.AppHost/realms/.
@Keycloak_HostAddress = https://localhost:8443
@Realm = fieldkit-dev

# The second tenant's realm — a different issuer and a different JWKS, validated by the same API.
@RealmB = fieldkit-dev-b

### Get an access token for the dev realm's fixture user.
### Direct access grant — a convenience for tooling. The browser uses authorization-code + PKCE, and
### real tenant realms turn this flow off (ADR-0008).
# @name token
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/{{Realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=fieldkit-web&username=rep&password=dev-only-not-a-secret

### Who does the API think I am? Requires a valid bearer token; 401 without one.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### The same endpoint with no token — expect 401 and a WWW-Authenticate challenge.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json

### Products — the classification tree, flat. The shape is built from `parentId`. Requires `product:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — create a root category. Requires `product:write`.
# @name beverages
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Beverages",
  "parentId": null
}

### Products — create a child of it.
# @name water
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Water",
  "parentId": "{{beverages.response.body.id}}"
}

### The same name under a *different* parent is fine — a tree disambiguates by position.
### Repeating it under Beverages is a 409 `product.category.nameTaken`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Water",
  "parentId": "{{beverages.response.body.id}}"
}

### Moving a category inside its own branch — expect 409 `product.category.cycle`.
### The first refusals in the API to carry an ADR-0012 `code` + `args` alongside the English
### `message`; everything else still answers with `message` only.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories/{{beverages.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Beverages",
  "parentId": "{{water.response.body.id}}"
}

### Deleting a category that still has children — expect 409 `product.category.hasChildren`,
### with the count in `args`. Nothing cascades.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/categories/{{beverages.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — the brands a tenant sells. Requires `product:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/brands
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — create a brand. Requires `product:write`.
# @name veridian
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/brands
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Veridian"
}

### The same name in different case — expect 409 `product.brand.nameTaken`.
### "Veridian" and "VERIDIAN" are one brand typed twice; letting both exist would mean every rule
### keyed to a brand quietly covers half the products.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/brands
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "VERIDIAN"
}

### Products — rename a brand. Requires `product:write`. The id survives, so every product filed
### under it stays filed: a rename corrects a label, it does not reclassify anything.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/brands/{{veridian.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Veridian Beverages"
}

### Products — tax classes. The classification only — standard, reduced, zero-rated — because a
### class is not a rate. Bottled water is reduced-rate in several European markets and
### standard-rated in others, and the percentage moves without the classification moving; folding
### them together would make a tenant selling in two countries pick which one to be wrong about.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — create a tax class. Requires `product:write`.
# @name reduced
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Reduced"
}

### What that class costs, per country (PRD-07). A PUT replaces the set. The window is half-open, so
### the old rate stops exactly where the new one starts — the changeover day is the one everybody
### looks at, and an inclusive end would leave it double-covered.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}/rates
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "rates": [
    { "countryCode": "RO", "percentage": "9",  "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01", "effectiveTo": "2026-07-01" },
    { "countryCode": "RO", "percentage": "11", "effectiveFrom": "2026-07-01" },
    { "countryCode": "DE", "percentage": "7",  "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01" }
  ]
}

### Read them back, ordered by country then start date.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}/rates
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A zero rate is **allowed**, unlike a zero discount. Zero-rated is how some goods are actually
### taxed, and forcing a tenant to express it by *omitting* a rate would make "no VAT here" and "we
### never set this up" the same state — one of which must never be assumed.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}/rates
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "rates": [
    { "countryCode": "RO", "percentage": "0", "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01" }
  ]
}

### A negative rate — expect 400 `product.tax.percentageOutOfRange` naming `rates[0].percentage`.
### A tax that pays the shop is a subsidy, and nothing downstream could sensibly handle one.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}/rates
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "rates": [
    { "countryCode": "RO", "percentage": "-1", "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01" }
  ]
}

### A country that is not a two-letter code — expect 400 `product.tax.countryInvalid`. Shape only,
### like a currency: what this refuses is "Romania", "ro " and "ROU".
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}/rates
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "rates": [
    { "countryCode": "Romania", "percentage": "19", "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01" }
  ]
}

### A blank name — expect 400 `product.taxClass.nameRequired`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "   "
}

### Products — rename a tax class. Requires `product:write`. The rates hang off the id, so renaming
### "Reduced" to "Reduced rate" leaves every percentage and window exactly where it was.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Reduced rate"
}

### Products — delete a tax class. Requires `product:write`. Refused with 409
### `product.taxClass.inUse` while any product is taxed as it, and the message names how many and
### what to do — "3 product(s) are taxed as 'Reduced rate'. Reclassify them first." is an
### instruction; "could not delete" is not.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/tax-classes/{{reduced.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — list products. Requires `product:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — create a product, unclassified. Requires `product:write`.
### The tenant is taken from the token's `tenant` claim; nothing in this request can influence it.
### All three classification ids are optional: a tenant can enter products before it has authored a
### brand list or a category tree.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-STL-050",
  "name": "Veridian Still 0.5L"
}

### Products — create a classified, described product, using the brand created above.
### `status` defaults to "Active"; `unitOfMeasure` and `packSize` are absent for anything sold loose.
# @name classified
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-STL-100",
  "name": "Veridian Still 1L",
  "brandId": "{{veridian.response.body.id}}",
  "unitOfMeasure": "CS",
  "packSize": 12
}

### Products — define a custom field for products, then set it on one.
### Same catalogue Outlets uses; `entity` is what keeps the two apart, so a field defined for
### outlets is an unknown key here rather than a valid value.
# @name productField
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "entity": "Product",
  "key": "shelf_life_days",
  "label": "Shelf life (days)",
  "type": "Number",
  "required": false,
  "minimum": 1
}

### Create a product carrying that value.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-STL-330",
  "name": "Veridian Still 330ml",
  "customFields": { "shelf_life_days": 540 }
}

### A key nobody defined — expect 400 `product.customField.unknown`, named
### `customFields.not_a_field`. Rejected rather than dropped: silently discarding it means an
### import loses data with no signal.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-BAD-003",
  "name": "Undefined field",
  "customFields": { "not_a_field": "x" }
}

### A value below the definition's minimum — expect 400 `product.customField.tooSmall`,
### with the bound in `args`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-BAD-004",
  "name": "Out of bounds",
  "customFields": { "shelf_life_days": 0 }
}

### A pack size below 1 — expect 400 `product.packSize.notPositive`.
### Refused rather than normalised to null: a pack of zero is not "no pack size", it is a typo, and
### quietly dropping it would let a bad import look like a deliberate omission.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-BAD-002",
  "name": "Pack of nothing",
  "packSize": 0
}

### Discontinue a product — and note it can be set back to "Active".
### Unlike an outlet closing, this is not a one-way door: seasonal lines come back, and forcing a new
### SKU to sell one again would orphan every order line that already points at this id.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/{{classified.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Veridian Still 1L",
  "brandId": "{{veridian.response.body.id}}",
  "unitOfMeasure": "CS",
  "packSize": 12,
  "status": "Discontinued"
}

### Three unknown classification ids — expect 400 with **three** problems, not one:
### `product.brand.missing`, `product.category.missing`, `product.taxClass.missing`.
### A form with three bad ids should be fixable in one pass rather than three round trips.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-BAD-001",
  "name": "Wrong on every count",
  "brandId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
  "categoryId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002",
  "taxClassId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003"
}

### Products — rename and reclassify. PUT replaces: omitting `brandId` *clears* the brand.
### The SKU is deliberately not editable — changing it is a different product, not a rename.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/{{classified.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Veridian Still 1L (renamed)"
}

### Deleting a brand a product is classified by — expect 409 `product.brand.inUse`,
### with the count in `args`. The foreign key would refuse it anyway; this says how many.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/brands/{{veridian.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — author a promotion (PRD-05). Two of B1's four types today: `PercentOff` and
### `FixedAmountOff`. Volume/tiered and BOGO need child rows of their own and are the second
### promotion PR.
###
### `value` is a **string**, like a price and for the same reason (`BR-PRD-8`) — "12.5% off" losing
### its last digit to a float is the same class of bug as a price doing so. It comes back formatted
### the way money is, so creating and reading spell it the same way.
###
### `priority` decides which promotion wins when several are in scope (`BR-PRD-3`): **higher beats
### lower**. The opposite convention ("priority 1 = first") means a deal that must beat everything
### already authored requires renumbering the rest, and eventually needs 0, then -1.
# @name percentOff
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Summer 15% — still water",
  "type": "PercentOff",
  "value": "15",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "validTo": "2026-09-01",
  "priority": 100
}

### Products — re-value, re-date and re-prioritise a promotion. Requires `product:write`.
### **No `type` and no `currency`**: the server reads both off the stored row. Re-typing would
### reinterpret the value — 15 meaning "15% off" becoming 15 meaning "€15 off" — and every order
### already priced against this rule would then be explained by a rule that no longer exists.
### Everything else is restated in full, because this is a PUT: a field left out is a field cleared.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Summer 12.5% — still water",
  "value": "12.5",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "validTo": "2026-09-01",
  "priority": 120
}

### The same PUT with a `value` a percentage cannot take — expect 400
### `product.promotion.percentOutOfRange`. Zero is refused alongside the negatives: a 0% promotion
### is not a rule that does nothing, it is a rule that wins a priority contest and *then* does
### nothing, which is worse than having no rule at all.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Summer 12.5% — still water",
  "value": "0",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "validTo": "2026-09-01",
  "priority": 120
}

### A fixed amount off, which carries **its own** currency rather than borrowing a price list's.
### A promotion is authored once and may reach outlets priced in more than one currency; refusing to
### discount a EUR line by an RON amount is `BR-PRD-1` holding, and PRD-06 can only make that check
### because the currency is stored here.
# @name amountOff
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Two-fifty off a case",
  "type": "FixedAmountOff",
  "value": "2.50",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "priority": 50
}

### List them, best priority first — the order someone reviewing a tenant's deals reads them in,
### and the order resolution will consider them.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### What it discounts. A PUT replaces the set. A category target is **not** expanded into its
### products: storing the expansion would freeze the category's membership at authoring time, so a
### product added to Beverages next week would silently miss a promotion that says it covers
### Beverages. Resolution walks the hierarchy instead (PRD-06).
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/targets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "productIds": ["{{classified.response.body.id}}"],
  "categoryIds": ["{{water.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Read the targets back.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/targets
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Withdraw it — an empty set is a real change, not a refusal. A promotion that targets nothing
### discounts nothing, which is how it is taken out of play without editing its window or deleting a
### record other things point at. The same shape and the same meaning as emptying a price list's
### assignments; two endpoints this alike should not disagree about what empty means.
###
### Nothing has to be guessed for this to be safe: resolution matches target rows, and no rows is no
### match. The dangerous reading — empty meaning *everything* — is not one anything here invites.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/targets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "productIds": [],
  "categoryIds": []
}

### 0% off — expect 400 `product.promotion.percentOutOfRange`. Zero is refused along with the
### negatives: it is not a rule that does nothing, it is a rule that will win a priority contest
### against a real discount and then take nothing off. Above 100 is refused too — that would make
### the line negative, turning a discount into a payment to the shop.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Nothing off",
  "type": "PercentOff",
  "value": "0",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01"
}

### A percentage carrying a currency — expect 400 `product.promotion.currencyNotApplicable`.
### Refused rather than ignored: silently dropping it means the author finds out when a report
### disagrees with what they thought they wrote.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Percent with money",
  "type": "PercentOff",
  "value": "15",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01"
}

### Products — a volume/tiered promotion: buy N or more, get more off (PRD-05). The **first type
### whose discount is not flat**, so it carries no `value` of its own — sending one is a 400
### (`product.promotion.valueNotApplicable`), because the caller has misunderstood where the
### discounts live and dropping it silently means they go on to author tiers they think are spare.
# @name tiered
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Buy more, pay less — still water",
  "type": "VolumeTiered",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "priority": 200
}

### Its thresholds. A PUT replaces the set, and the bound is **inclusive with no upper end** — a tier
### is "N or more", and resolution takes the highest threshold the quantity reaches. That is why
### tiers never state where they stop, and so cannot leave a gap by disagreeing about it: the same
### instinct as the half-open date window, one dimension over.
###
### Deliberately sent out of order; they come back ascending.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": [
    { "minQuantity": 24, "value": "10" },
    { "minQuantity": 6,  "value": "2.5" },
    { "minQuantity": 12, "value": "5" }
  ]
}

### Read them back — ascending, and each value spelled the way money is.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Amount tiers instead, each carrying its currency. One currency across the set (`BR-PRD-1`):
### EUR at one threshold and RON at another cannot be compared or summed, and resolution would have
### to pick a currency nobody declared. Expect 400 `product.promotion.tierCurrenciesMixed` if they
### disagree.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": [
    { "minQuantity": 6,  "value": "1.50", "currency": "EUR" },
    { "minQuantity": 12, "value": "4.00", "currency": "EUR" }
  ]
}

### Mixed kinds — expect 400 `product.promotion.tierKindsMixed`. Nothing about resolution requires
### the rule: tiers are picked by quantity and never compared to each other, so a mixed set is
### perfectly well-defined. It is refused because "5% off at 6, three euros off at 12" is a set
### nobody can sanity-check at a glance, and is far likelier to be a slip than an intention.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": [
    { "minQuantity": 6,  "value": "5" },
    { "minQuantity": 12, "value": "3.00", "currency": "EUR" }
  ]
}

### A tier starting at 1, and a second one out of range — expect
### `product.promotion.tierQuantityTooSmall` naming `tiers[0].minQuantity` and
### `product.promotion.percentOutOfRange` naming `tiers[1].value`. The index is part of the answer:
### a form showing four tiers cannot work out from "is above 0 and at most 100" which row to
### highlight. "Buy one or more" is every line that matched at all — a flat discount wearing a
### tier's clothes, silently shadowing the `PercentOff` type it duplicates.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": [
    { "minQuantity": 1,  "value": "5" },
    { "minQuantity": 12, "value": "150" }
  ]
}

### Withdraw it — an empty set, like an empty target set and like a price list with no assignments.
### Three endpoints of the same shape, one answer to what empty means.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": []
}

### Tiers on a flat promotion — expect 400 `product.promotion.tiersNotApplicable`. It would then
### hold two discounts with no rule saying which applies.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/tiers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "tiers": [
    { "minQuantity": 6, "value": "5" }
  ]
}

### Products — buy X get Y (PRD-05). The last of B1's four types, and the only one that does not
### reduce a price: it gives units. That is why it carries quantities instead of a `value`, and why
### PRD-06 will have to add an order line rather than adjust one.
###
### `getPercentOff: "100"` is free — the classic BOGO. 100 is not a special case in the storage, only
### in what a shopper calls it, which is what lets "get one free" and "get one half price" be the
### same offer with a different number rather than two types.
###
### `getProductId` omitted means **the same product that was bought**. That matters when the targets
### are a whole category: "buy two of anything in Beverages, get one free" gives one of whichever was
### bought, and there is no single id to write down.
# @name bogo
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Buy two, get one free — still water",
  "type": "BuyXGetY",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "priority": 300,
  "bundle": {
    "buyQuantity": 2,
    "getQuantity": 1,
    "getPercentOff": "100"
  }
}

### The same mechanism with the id filled in — a cross-sell bundle rather than a second type.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Buy three cases, get a crate half price",
  "type": "BuyXGetY",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "priority": 250,
  "bundle": {
    "buyQuantity": 3,
    "getQuantity": 1,
    "getPercentOff": "50",
    "getProductId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}"
  }
}

### Read it back — the bundle comes back as one nested object, not four loose fields. Four properties
### that are only ever all-set or all-null belong together in the shape a caller reads, so a client
### asks `bundle == null` instead of checking four things that have to agree. Every other type
### answers `"bundle": null`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{bogo.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### No bundle at all — expect 400 `product.promotion.bundleRequired`. Required, unlike tiers and
### targets, which may be empty to mean "reaches nobody": an empty set is still a coherent promotion,
### one that discounts nothing, whereas "buy ? get ?" is not a rule at all.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Buy nothing in particular",
  "type": "BuyXGetY",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01"
}

### Nothing on one side — expect 400 `product.promotion.bundleQuantityTooSmall` naming
### `bundle.getQuantity`. "Buy two get none" does nothing while still winning a priority contest
### against a rule that would have; "buy none get one" gives the product to anyone who orders
### anything.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Buy two get nothing",
  "type": "BuyXGetY",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "bundle": { "buyQuantity": 2, "getQuantity": 0, "getPercentOff": "100" }
}

### A bundle on a type that does not give units — expect 400
### `product.promotion.bundleNotApplicable`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Percent with a bundle",
  "type": "PercentOff",
  "value": "15",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "bundle": { "buyQuantity": 2, "getQuantity": 1, "getPercentOff": "100" }
}

### Four things wrong at once — expect `product.promotion.nameRequired`,
### `product.promotion.percentOutOfRange`, `product.promotion.currencyNotApplicable` and
### `product.promotion.windowInverted`, all in one envelope. Equal dates are an inverted window
### because the range is half-open: a promotion that is never live.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "   ",
  "type": "PercentOff",
  "value": "150",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "validFrom": "2026-06-01",
  "validTo": "2026-06-01"
}

### Same create, anonymous — expect 401.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sku": "VRD-STL-051",
  "name": "Should not be created"
}

### A token for `viewer`, who holds product:read but not product:write.
# @name viewerToken
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/{{Realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=fieldkit-web&username=viewer&password=dev-only-not-a-secret

### Create as `viewer` — expect 403 (authenticated, but lacking the permission), not 401.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "VRD-STL-052",
  "name": "Should be forbidden"
}

### A token for `admin`, who holds the IAM permissions and none of Catalog's.
# @name adminToken
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/{{Realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=fieldkit-web&username=admin&password=dev-only-not-a-secret

### The permission catalogue — every permission the running system enforces, contributed by the
### modules that own them. There is no endpoint to add one: a permission nothing checks is not a
### permission. Requires `role:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/permissions
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### List roles. Requires `role:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Create a role. Requires `role:write`.
# @name newRole
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Field Rep",
  "permissions": ["product:read"]
}

### Replace a role's permissions. Wholesale, not a patch.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles/{{newRole.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Field Rep",
  "permissions": ["product:read", "product:write"]
}

### A permission no module enforces — expect 400 naming the unknown entry. This is what the
### catalogue is for: `prodcut:read` would otherwise store fine and grant nothing.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Typo Role",
  "permissions": ["prodcut:read"]
}

### Roles as `rep` — authenticated, holds both product permissions, still 403. Capabilities are
### independent: being trusted with products grants nothing over who may use them.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### List users. Requires `user:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Create a user profile. Requires `user:write`.
### Profile only — this does NOT create the Keycloak account. Realm/account provisioning is IAM-10;
### until then an operator creates the Keycloak user and `subjectId` links the two.
# @name newUser
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "subjectId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000abcd",
  "email": "maria@veridian.example",
  "displayName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "locale": "ro-RO",
  "timeZone": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "roleIds": ["{{newRole.response.body.id}}"]
}

### A user with no roles — expect 400. BR-IAM-3: a user who can authenticate and do nothing reads
### as a broken account, not a disabled one. Deactivate instead.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "subjectId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000dead",
  "email": "roleless@veridian.example",
  "displayName": "No Roles",
  "locale": "ro-RO",
  "timeZone": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "roleIds": []
}

### An unknown time zone — expect 400. Validated against the runtime, not merely required: an
### unknown zone fails at render time in front of a rep instead of here in front of an admin.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "subjectId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000beef",
  "email": "badzone@veridian.example",
  "displayName": "Bad Zone",
  "locale": "ro-RO",
  "timeZone": "Mars/Olympus_Mons",
  "roleIds": ["{{newRole.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Deactivate. Publishes UserDeactivated through the outbox so Sync releases the bound device (A8).
### Idempotent: a second call changes nothing and publishes nothing.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users/{{newUser.response.body.id}}/deactivate
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Reactivate.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users/{{newUser.response.body.id}}/reactivate
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Delete a role. Refused for a system template, or while users still hold it.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles/{{newRole.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The roles a tenant is seeded with (IAM-06) — Field Rep, Supervisor, Sales Ops, Tenant Admin,
### each flagged `isSystemTemplate`. A tenant without them has permissions nobody can hold.
# @name seededRoles
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Delete a system template — expect 409. Templates are the way back to a working set of roles, so
### they may be renamed and recomposed but not removed. (Take an id from the response above.)
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles/{{seededRoles.response.body.$[0].id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### ─── Organization · the sales hierarchy (ORG-01) ────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Every org unit for the tenant, flat — the tree is `parentId`, not nesting. Requires `orgunit:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A root unit (no parent). Requires `orgunit:write`.
# @name country
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Romania",
  "parentId": null
}

### A child. Depth is whatever the tenant builds — nothing in the schema knows these labels.
# @name region
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Bucharest & South",
  "parentId": "{{country.response.body.id}}"
}

### A grandchild.
# @name team
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Team North",
  "parentId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### A sibling with a name already taken — expect 409. Names are unique among siblings only, so the
### same "Team North" under a different region is fine.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Team North",
  "parentId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### Rename and reparent in one call — one edit on one screen, so one request that cannot half-apply.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units/{{team.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Team North-East",
  "parentId": "{{country.response.body.id}}"
}

### Move a unit into its own branch — expect 409. Nothing in the database can catch this: every
### parent still exists, but the branch detaches from every root and no traversal reaches it again.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units/{{country.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Romania",
  "parentId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### Delete a unit that still has children — expect 409, not a cascade. Removing a region should not
### silently take its areas and teams with it.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units/{{country.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The hierarchy as `viewer`, who holds orgunit:read and not orgunit:write — 200 here…
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

### …and 403 here. Reading the org chart and redrawing it are different capabilities.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Should be forbidden",
  "parentId": null
}

### ─── Organization · positions and the management line (ORG-02) ──────────────────────────────────

### Who is in the hierarchy. Optional `?orgUnitId=` narrows to one unit. Requires `position:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Put someone in the tree. The user is resolved through IAM's IUserDirectory contract, so this
### 400s for a subject id this tenant does not have. `title` is a label — never an authorization
### input (BR-IAM-2); what the person may do comes from their token.
# @name position
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}",
  "title": "Supervisor"
}

### The same user in the same unit twice — expect 409. Across *different* units is allowed:
### covering two areas is an ordinary arrangement.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}",
  "title": "Supervisor again"
}

### Retitle. Moving someone is deliberately *not* possible here — it changes who they report
### through and what they see, so it is a delete and a create, and the audit trail shows both.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions/{{position.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}",
  "title": "Senior Supervisor"
}

### Move by editing — expect 400 naming the delete-and-create path.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions/{{position.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "orgUnitId": "{{country.response.body.id}}",
  "title": "Senior Supervisor"
}

### What the hierarchy means for one person. `managementLine` runs *up* (who they report through,
### for roll-up reporting); `visibleUnitIds` runs *down* (BR-ORG-4's visibility scope). Returned as
### data — enforcing it is ORG-09.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/users/{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}/scope
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Delete the unit someone occupies — expect 409, not a silent removal from the org chart.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/units/{{region.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Staffing as `viewer`, who holds position:read and not position:write — 403.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/positions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}",
  "title": "Should be forbidden"
}

### ─── Outlets · the base and its classification (OUT-01, OUT-04) ─────────────────────────────────

### The trade classifications this tenant works with. Requires `channel:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A channel. Reference data with a stable id rather than a string on the outlet: assortment and
### pricing key off the id, so the label can be corrected without any rule silently ceasing to match.
# @name channel
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Modern Trade"
}

### An outlet. `code` is the tenant's own identifier — what the location is called in their ERP —
### and a channel is mandatory (BR-OUT-1): without one there is no assortment, price list or visit
### workflow to give it.
# @name outlet
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0001",
  "name": "Mega Image Dorobanți",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "segment": "A",
  "banner": "Mega Image",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "address": {
    "street": "Calea Dorobanți 172",
    "city": "București",
    "postalCode": "010578",
    "countryCode": "RO"
  },
  "location": { "latitude": 44.4638, "longitude": 26.0946 },
  "contacts": [
    { "name": "Ana Ionescu", "role": "Store manager", "phone": "+40 721 000 000", "email": "ana@example.ro" }
  ]
}

### Products — set a channel's assortment. A PUT **replaces** it: an assortment is a set, and a
### partial update of a set has no obvious meaning. `mustStock` flags the MSL subset (`B2`).
### Requires `product:write`.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/channels/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "items": [
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "mustStock": true }
  ]
}

### Read it back — must-stock first, so a suggested-order list needs no second sort.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/channels/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A channel that does not exist — expect 400 `product.assortment.channelMissing`.
### Products cannot see the channel table (AT-1), so it asks Outlets through
### `IOutletClassification.ChannelExistsAsync`. Without that check this would save cleanly and simply
### never apply to anybody.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/channels/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "items": []
}

### The **effective** assortment for one outlet: its channel's list, plus what this shop adds, minus
### what it removes. Computed on read rather than stored, so a change to the channel assortment is
### immediately true everywhere without a backfill that can half-fail.
###
### The channel join is what Products cannot make alone — it knows which channel an assortment is
### for, and only Outlets knows which channel this shop is. An unknown outlet is 404, not an empty
### list: "no such shop" and "nothing is sold there" are different answers.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### This outlet refuses a line its channel carries. A PUT replaces all of its overrides.
### `kind` is "Added" or "Removed"; `mustStock` applies only to "Added", since a product added for
### one shop still has to say whether it is expected on that shelf.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/overrides
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "overrides": [
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "kind": "Removed" }
  ]
}

### Read the overrides back — the departures themselves, not the effective list.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/overrides
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### The same product added *and* removed — expect 400 `product.assortment.duplicateOverride`.
### A shop where one product is both has no answer, and every read would have to pick one.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/assortments/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/overrides
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "overrides": [
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "kind": "Added" },
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "kind": "Removed" }
  ]
}

### Products — create a price list. One currency for the whole list (`BR-PRD-1`), and a half-open
### window `[effectiveFrom, effectiveTo)` so a successor starts exactly where this stops.
### `effectiveTo` may be omitted for an open-ended list.
# @name priceList
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Modern Trade 2026",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01"
}

### Products — list price lists. Requires `product:read`. Without their prices: authoring a list and
### pricing several hundred products into one are different sittings, so the index stays small.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — rename a list and move its window. Requires `product:write`. **No `currency`** — the
### API has no parameter for it, because changing it would reinterpret every amount in the list
### rather than convert one: 12.50 EUR becoming 12.50 RON is not a conversion. A tenant needing the
### same prices in another currency needs another list, priced for it.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Modern Trade 2026 (H1)",
  "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01",
  "effectiveTo": "2026-07-01"
}

### Set the prices. Amounts are **strings** — `BR-PRD-8`. A JSON number becomes an IEEE-754 float
### the moment a browser parses it, and the device pricing engine would be doing float maths before
### `decimal.js` ever saw the value.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/prices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "prices": [
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "amount": "12.50" }
  ]
}

### Read them back — each price comes out as `{ "amount": "12.50", "currency": "EUR" }`.
### The currency comes from the list, not the line, so no two lines can disagree about units.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/prices
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A comma decimal — expect 400 `product.price.notANumber`. Under invariant culture "12,50" would
### parse to **1250** if thousands separators were allowed: a hundredfold error that reads as a
### plausible price, and only on machines configured a certain way.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/prices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "prices": [
    { "productId": "{{classified.response.body.id}}", "amount": "12,50" }
  ]
}

### Where the list applies. A PUT replaces the whole scope — channels and outlets together.
### Each stored row is scoped to exactly one of the two, and the database enforces that with a check
### constraint rather than trusting this endpoint.
###
### Publishing here also raises `PriceListPublished` into the outbox, in the same transaction as the
### rows (ADR-0006), which is what Sync will turn into a reference delta.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": ["{{channel.response.body.id}}"],
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Read the scope back.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/assignments
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — what this outlet is charged, on a date (PRD-04, BR-PRD-2). Requires `product:read`:
### a rep prices an order, and needing `product:write` for that would let the whole field force
### reprice the tenant.
###
### The list above reaches this outlet twice — once through its channel, once directly — so the
### answer names which one won. `scope: "Outlet"` here; the outlet-scoped assignment beats the
### channel one even though both say 12.50, because specificity is decided before amount.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/prices?on=2026-03-15
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Narrowed to particular products. Repeat `productId` per product — a rep pricing an order asks
### about the lines they are taking, not the whole catalogue.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/prices?on=2026-03-15&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A date before the list opens — expect `[]`, not an error. A product with no covering list is a
### product this outlet cannot be sold, which is the caller's decision rather than a refusal.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/prices?on=2025-12-31
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### No date at all — expect 400 `product.price.dateRequired`. Deliberately not defaulted to today:
### that would mean the *server's* today, and an outlet in Bucharest changes day six hours before one
### in London (BR-PRD-6). It would also make the endpoint non-reproducible, when the whole point is
### that an order re-priced during sync resolves to the price it was taken at.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/prices
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A date that is not one — expect 400 `product.price.dateMalformed`, naming `on`. Hand-parsed
### rather than bound as a `DateOnly?`, so this is a field problem like every other refusal here
### instead of the framework's bare 400 with no body.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/prices?on=15-03-2026
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### An outlet that does not exist — expect 404. Another tenant's outlet gives the same answer:
### `IOutletClassification` is tenant-filtered, so it is simply absent here, which is the only reply
### that does not confirm it exists elsewhere.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009/prices?on=2026-03-15
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Withdraw it — an empty scope is a real change, not a no-op. It is how a list stops applying, and
### it raises `PriceListPublished` too: a device that never hears this keeps pricing against a list
### that no longer reaches it.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": [],
  "outletIds": []
}

### A channel that does not exist — expect 400 `product.priceList.channelMissing`.
### Products cannot see the channel table (AT-1), so this is checked through
### `IOutletClassification.ChannelExistsAsync`; without it the assignment would save and price nobody.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": ["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009"],
  "outletIds": []
}

### A currency that is not a three-letter code — expect 400 `product.priceList.currencyInvalid`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Wrong currency",
  "currency": "Euro",
  "effectiveFrom": "2026-01-01"
}

### Products — where a promotion applies (PRD-05). The last thing it needs before it does anything:
### type, value, targets and window all describe a rule, and this says who it happens to. Until it is
### set, a promotion is a draft that discounts nobody — the same intermediate state a price list
### passes through.
###
### These requests live here rather than beside the rest of the promotion block because they depend
### on `channel` and `outlet`, which are created above. Sent from up there they would resolve to
### nothing, which is the ordering bug #109 fixed for the price-list scope requests.
###
### A PUT replaces the whole scope, and raises `PromotionActivated` into the outbox in the same
### transaction as the rows (ADR-0006) — the event the module registry names, and what Sync will turn
### into a reference delta.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": ["{{channel.response.body.id}}"],
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Read the scope back.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/assignments
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Reach is a property of a promotion, not of its type — a tiered deal is pointed at outlets exactly
### as a flat percentage is.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{tiered.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": ["{{channel.response.body.id}}"],
  "outletIds": []
}

### And so is a BOGO.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{bogo.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": [],
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Withdraw it — an empty scope raises `PromotionActivated` too, with both counts at zero. "This
### promotion now reaches nobody" is a change a consumer needs as much as any other: a device that
### never hears it keeps offering a deal that has been pulled.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": [],
  "outletIds": []
}

### A channel that does not exist — expect 400 `product.promotion.channelMissing`. Products cannot
### see the channel table (AT-1), so this goes through `IOutletClassification.ChannelExistsAsync`;
### without it the scope would save cleanly and reach nobody.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/promotions/{{percentOff.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "channelIds": ["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009"],
  "outletIds": []
}

### Products — which promotion applies to one order line (PRD-04's sibling, PRD-06). At most **one**
### per line, selected by **priority** (`BR-PRD-3`) — deliberately not by the size of the discount,
### so a supplier-funded deal can be made to beat a bigger generic one. That is the whole reason
### priority is authored, and a resolver that quietly preferred the larger saving would look right on
### most data and be wrong exactly when it mattered.
###
### `on` is required for the same two reasons as on `/prices`: a default would mean the *server's*
### today, and `BR-PRD-6` evaluates a window in the **outlet's** timezone — so the business date is
### computed where the timezone is known and handed in. It also keeps resolution reproducible, which
### is what lets an order re-priced during sync select the promotion it was taken under.
###
### `quantity` is required too, and that one matters more than it looks: left out it would bind to 0,
### and a line of zero reaches no tier and earns no bundle — so every volume deal and every BOGO
### would silently resolve to nothing, reading as "this shop has no promotions" rather than as a
### mistake.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/promotions?on=2026-06-15&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}&quantity=1
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### The same line at 30. A tiered promotion resolves to **the tier the quantity reaches** — highest
### threshold at or below it, since tiers are "N or more" — and comes back already collapsed to a
### concrete discount rather than a table to search again.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/promotions?on=2026-06-15&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}&quantity=30
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### A date the promotions do not cover — expect `{ "promotion": null }`, a stated answer rather than
### a 404 or an empty body. "No promotion applies" is a fact about this line, not a missing resource.
### The wrapper exists because ASP.NET Core writes *nothing* for a null result value, and an empty
### body is not JSON.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/promotions?on=2025-12-31&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}&quantity=1
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### No quantity — expect 400 `product.promotion.quantityRequired`. A quantity of 0 is refused
### separately, as `product.promotion.quantityTooSmall`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/promotions?on=2026-06-15&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### Products — which tax rate applies to a product at this outlet (PRD-07). The **country comes from
### the outlet's address**, through `IOutletClassification` — Products cannot see the address table
### (AT-1), and having the caller pass a country would invite the client and the server to disagree
### about which jurisdiction a shop is in.
###
### Expect `{ "tax": null }` here unless the product carries a tax class *and* a rate is authored for
### its country on this date. **Null is not zero.** It means the tax is unknown — the outlet has no
### country, the product has no class, or nobody authored a rate — and a caller that treats it as
### zero invoices untaxed while looking deliberate about it. That is precisely why a 0% rate is
### separately authorable: zero-rated and never-set-up must stay distinguishable.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/tax?on=2026-06-15&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### The day a rate change takes over. The outlet above is in RO, so with the two Romanian rates
### authored earlier this answers 9.00 on 2026-06-30 and 11.00 on 2026-07-01 — the half-open window
### doing its job on the only day anyone checks.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/tax?on=2026-06-30&productId={{classified.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### No product — expect 400 `product.tax.productRequired`. Tax follows the product's classification,
### so there is nothing to answer without one.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/tax?on=2026-06-15
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### An unknown time zone — expect 400. Checked against the runtime's zone database rather than a
### pattern, because "Europe/Bucuresti" is well-formed and does not exist. A visit's business day and
### a promotion's validity both resolve in this zone, so a wrong one is a wrong answer waiting.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0003",
  "name": "Bad zone",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucuresti"
}

### A point that is not on the earth — expect 400, always. Whether a coordinate is *valid* is data
### integrity, not a tenant policy: latitude 91 is meaningless for every kind of outlet and every
### kind of visit. Whether a rep must be *standing at* it is policy, and lives in Visit — BR-VIS-2
### allows an out-of-geofence check-in with a recorded override reason, and never blocks the rep.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0004",
  "name": "Off earth",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "location": { "latitude": 91, "longitude": 200 }
}

### An outlet with no coordinates at all — accepted. They are optional; onboarding data routinely
### arrives without them.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0005",
  "name": "No coordinates",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### Contacts are replaced wholesale, so an empty list erases them. They are personal data (B8) and
### this is the deletion path today — the rows go, rather than being flagged.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Mega Image Dorobanți",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "contacts": []
}

### The same code twice — expect 409. Unique per tenant, not globally: two tenants numbering their
### stores from 1 is the ordinary case, not a collision.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0001",
  "name": "Duplicate",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### An unknown channel — expect 400.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0002",
  "name": "No channel",
  "channelId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### Edit the details. Note there is no `code` field: the code is how an import recognises a location
### it has already seen, so an edit that could change it would make the next import create a
### duplicate instead of updating the original.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Mega Image Dorobanți 1",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "segment": "B",
  "banner": "Mega Image",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### Filter the base. The two questions a back office actually asks of it, both indexed.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?channelId={{channel.response.body.id}}&status=Active
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Status is its own endpoint, not a field on the edit: "this store is shut" is a different
### decision from "the name was spelled wrong", and merging them lets a careless PUT close an outlet
### as a side effect of fixing a typo.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "status": "Inactive"
}

### Closing without a reason — expect 400. Closing is irreversible and removes the outlet from every
### future journey, so "why" is the question an auditor will ask, and the person who can answer it is
### the one doing it now. A routine Active↔Inactive toggle does not need one.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "status": "Closed"
}

### Closing is permanent (OUT-04) — it is what makes Closed mean more than a long-lived Inactive,
### and what BR-OUT-4's "excluded from new journeys, keeps its history" rests on.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "status": "Closed",
  "reason": "Lease ended; site redeveloped"
}

### The audit trail: every transition, newest first, starting with the outlet's creation (`from` is
### null). Append-only — there is deliberately no endpoint to add, edit or remove an entry, because
### an audit log with a write path is one that can be arranged after the fact.
###
### Each entry carries both `changedBy` (the Keycloak subject) and `changedByName`, resolved through
### `IUserDirectory` at read time. Both, because a display name is a mutable label while the subject
### is the identity — a rename must not rewrite who did what in March. `changedByName` is null when
### the subject matches no user in this tenant, which is ordinary: a deleted account, an import
### principal, or a subject that predates the user record. Resolved server-side because reading this
### trail needs only `outlet:read`, while the user list needs `user:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status-history
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The same trail as a caller who may read outlets and nothing else — the case that decides where
### the name is resolved. `changedByName` is still populated; a client-side join would show this
### reader raw GUIDs.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status-history
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Reopening a closed outlet — expect 409 naming the new-outlet path.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "status": "Active"
}

### Delete a channel that outlets are classified as — expect 409. BR-OUT-1 means there is no such
### thing as removing a channel from under the outlets that use it.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The base as `rep`, who holds outlet:read + channel:read and neither write — 200 here…
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### …and 403 here. Walking the round and owning the master data are different capabilities.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Should be forbidden"
}

### ─── Organization · territories and their outlets (ORG-03, ORG-05) ──────────────────────────────

### Territories, with how many outlets each holds. Requires `territory:read`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A territory belongs to an org unit, and that is required: BR-ORG-4 says a supervisor sees the
### territories under their branch, so one under no branch would be visible to nobody.
# @name territory
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Bucharest North",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### Assign outlets. Validated through the Outlets module's IOutletCatalog contract — Organization
### never reads the outlets schema — so an id this tenant does not have is rejected, and the whole
### batch is refused rather than half-applied.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### The same outlet again — expect 204, not an error. Idempotent, so a retry after a dropped
### response does the right thing.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### The outlets in a territory, named through the contract. An outlet that no longer resolves keeps
### its row with nulls rather than disappearing — the membership is real, and hiding it would make
### the territory quietly smaller than the count on the list screen says.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A second territory, to demonstrate single-primary.
# @name otherTerritory
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Bucharest South",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### The same outlet in a second territory — expect 409 (BR-ORG-1 / ORG-05). Refused rather than
### moved silently: a territory's membership is a rep's offline data scope (BR-ORG-3), so this
### changes what somebody's device downloads tomorrow. Remove it from the first one first.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{otherTerritory.response.body.id}}/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Remove an outlet from a territory — the first half of a move.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Delete a territory that still holds outlets — expect 409, not a cascade. Every outlet in it
### would silently become unassigned.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{otherTerritory.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Territories as `viewer`, who holds territory:read and not territory:write — 403 on the write.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Should be forbidden",
  "orgUnitId": "{{region.response.body.id}}"
}

### ─── Organization · rep assignments (ORG-04) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Assign a rep to a territory for a period. Dates are inclusive at both ends, and omitting `to`
### means "until further notice". The rep is resolved through IAM's IUserDirectory contract.
# @name assignment
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-01",
  "to": "2026-03-20"
}

### An overlapping period — expect 409 (BR-ORG-2). Sharing even a single day would mean two reps
### covering one territory that day; the response names the assignment in the way.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-20",
  "to": "2026-03-25"
}

### Starting the day after — the handover case, and allowed.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-21",
  "to": "2026-03-31"
}

### Ending before it starts — expect 400.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/assignments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-04-20",
  "to": "2026-04-10"
}

### What covers this territory, and when. `isCurrent` is resolved in the *calling* user's timezone —
### a territory spans outlets in different zones, so there is no "territory time".
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/assignments
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The same question from the rep's side — BR-ORG-3's offline scope, which Sync will ask on pull.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/users/{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}/assignments
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Edit in place. Fully editable, subject to the overlap rule — correcting a mistyped start date
### should not need a cancellation and a replacement. Publishes RepAssignmentChanged either way.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/assignments/{{assignment.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.request.body.$.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-02",
  "to": "2026-03-20"
}

### Remove it. The event is published *before* the row goes, while the module still knows whose
### device needs to shrink.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/assignments/{{assignment.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### ─── Multi-issuer (ADR-0008) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### A token from the SECOND tenant's realm. Different issuer, different signing keys, same API.
# @name tenantBToken
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/{{RealmB}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=fieldkit-web&username=rep-b&password=dev-only-not-a-secret

### Expect 200 with tenant `…0002`. The realm the token came from is what decides that — nothing in
### the request can influence it.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantBToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Products as tenant B. Expect NOT to see anything created above with `rep`'s token, and to see
### only what B created — the same query filter, driven by a genuinely different issuer.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantBToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A token from realm B's `fieldkit-impostor` client, whose hardcoded `tenant` claim names the
### FIRST tenant. Properly signed by a trusted issuer, asserting a tenant that issuer does not own.
# @name impostorToken
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/{{RealmB}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=fieldkit-impostor&username=rep-b&password=dev-only-not-a-secret

### Expect 401. The token's `tenant` claim must match the tenant that owns its issuer; without that
### comparison this request would return tenant A's data to tenant B's realm.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{impostorToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A real, correctly-signed token from Keycloak's own `master` realm — which no tenant row claims.
### Expect 401: the tenant table is the trust list, so realm-creation rights are not tenant-creation
### rights. (Uses the Aspire-generated admin credentials from user-secrets.)
# @name untrustedRealmToken
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=admin-cli&username={{$dotenv KEYCLOAK_ADMIN}}&password={{$dotenv KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD}}

###
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{untrustedRealmToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

### ─── Configuration: the custom-field catalogue (CFG-01, CFG-02) ──────────────────────────────────

### What this tenant may record about an outlet. Renders the back-office form, so `config:read` is
### held by every role that opens an outlet screen — reading the catalogue is not an admin act.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions?entity=Outlet
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A number with bounds. `key` is an identifier and is immutable after creation: it is the JSONB
### key already written into every outlet row, so renaming it would orphan the values.
# @name chillerField
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "entity": "Outlet",
  "key": "chiller_count",
  "label": "Chiller count",
  "type": "Number",
  "required": false,
  "minimum": 0,
  "maximum": 50
}

### A closed vocabulary. Options are compared ordinally — "Franchise" is not "franchise", because a
### tenant that meant them as the same value should have defined one of them.
# @name ownershipField
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "entity": "Outlet",
  "key": "ownership",
  "label": "Ownership",
  "type": "Choice",
  "required": false,
  "options": ["independent", "franchise", "cooperative"]
}

### Expect 400. A choice with no options can never be satisfied — it would save happily and then
### reject every value, with the failure looking like the value's fault.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "entity": "Outlet",
  "key": "unsatisfiable",
  "label": "Unsatisfiable",
  "type": "Choice"
}

### Relabel it. The label is what an admin sees; the key is what the data is stored under, and only
### one of those is safe to change.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions/{{chillerField.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "label": "Chillers in store",
  "type": "Number",
  "required": false,
  "minimum": 0,
  "maximum": 80
}

### An outlet carrying values the catalogue describes. Outlets does not know what `ownership` means —
### it asks Configuration, which is the whole point of ADR-0009's typed-JSONB shape.
# @name customOutlet
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "OUT-CFG-1",
  "name": "Alimentara Central",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "customFields": {
    "chiller_count": 3,
    "ownership": "franchise"
  }
}

### Expect 400 — a key no definition describes. Rejected rather than dropped: silently discarding it
### would lose an import's data with no signal.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "OUT-CFG-2",
  "name": "Unknown field",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "customFields": { "not_defined_anywhere": "value" }
}

### Expect 400 — over the maximum the definition set. The server re-validates whatever the device
### believed (BR-CFG-3).
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "OUT-CFG-3",
  "name": "Out of range",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "customFields": { "chiller_count": 900 }
}

### Values are replaced wholesale, not patched — an empty map is the only way to clear an optional
### field, and a PUT that omits `customFields` means "none", same as every other field on this body.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{customOutlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Alimentara Central",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "customFields": { "ownership": "independent" }
}

### As tenant B: the definitions above are invisible, so the same payload is refused for naming a key
### that — from where B stands — does not exist. Nothing in Outlets had to know about tenants.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions?entity=Outlet
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantBToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Delete a definition. The values already written stay in the JSONB — this stops the field being
### collected, it does not rewrite history.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions/{{ownershipField.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###
### ─── A body the server cannot read (api-contracts §3.1) ─────────────────────────────────────────

### Expect 400, not 500. An enum travels by name, so a name that is not one of the names is the
### caller's mistake — and `detail` names the path (`$.status`) rather than the .NET type the parser
### failed to build.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "status": "Nonsense",
  "reason": "not one of Active, Inactive, Closed"
}

### The same shape in another module — the fix is the host's, so every enum-carrying endpoint has it.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/field-definitions
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "entity": "Outlet",
  "key": "k",
  "label": "L",
  "type": "Sasquatch"
}

### Expect 400 — not JSON at all.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{ this is not json

### Expect 400 — valid JSON, wrong shape entirely.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/channels
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

["not", "an", "object"]

### Expect 400 — a body the server reads perfectly and then finds a hole in. `permissions` is
### declared non-nullable, so leaving it out is the caller's mistake; before the host set
### RespectRequiredConstructorParameters it deserialized to null and the handler's first
### `.Where(...)` was a NullReferenceException, which is to say a 500 blaming the server for a field
### the caller never sent. Nine endpoints across IAM and Products answered that way.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/roles
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "Supervisor"
}

### The same hole one level down — `{}` to a PUT that replaces a set. Note what this is *not*: an
### omitted array does not mean "leave the prices alone", and it is refused rather than read as an
### empty set, because the two differ by every price on the list.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products/price-lists/{{priceList.response.body.id}}/prices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### Expect 400 `product.name.tooLong`, with `max` and `length` in `args` so a form can say both what
### the limit is and how far over it the caller went. This was a DbUpdateException and a 500 — the
### column is varchar(200) and nothing checked before the write, so the API reported a typed name as
### a server fault.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "sku": "SKU-TOO-LONG-1",
  "name": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
}

### Expect 400 `outlet.countryCode.invalid`. Refused rather than truncated: "Romania" cut to "Ro"
### fits the column and then matches no tax rate for the rest of the outlet's life.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0200",
  "name": "Country Spelled Out",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "address": { "countryCode": "Romania" }
}

### And a well-formed one is stored upper-cased — send "ro", read back "RO". The bug this replaces
### was silent in the worst way: TaxRate.Create upper-cases its country, the outlet's was stored as
### typed, and resolution compares the two directly. So "ro" matched no rate, tax resolution answered
### "no tax", and that is indistinguishable from a tax class nobody has priced — the very distinction
### PRD-07 exists to keep. Nothing errored and nothing logged.
# @name lowerCaseCountry
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0201",
  "name": "Lower Case Country",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "address": { "street": "Str. Dorobanti 1", "city": "Bucharest", "postalCode": "010001", "countryCode": "ro" }
}

### It survives an update typed the same way — the normalisation is on the write path, not on create.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{lowerCaseCountry.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "{{lowerCaseCountry.response.body.name}}",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "address": { "street": "Str. Dorobanti 1", "city": "Bucharest", "postalCode": "010001", "countryCode": "ro" }
}

###

### ─── Bulk import of outlets (OUT-05) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Dry run first — the natural first click in the import screen. Validates everything, writes
### nothing, and answers "what would happen" before anything does.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import?dryRun=true
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone,city,latitude,longitude
IMP-1,Alimentara Central,{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,Bucharest,44.4268,26.1025
IMP-2,"Smith, Jones & Co",{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,Cluj-Napoca,46.7712,23.6236

### The same file for real. The body *is* the file and its content type chooses the reader — which is
### how JSON and Excel will slot in beside CSV rather than needing their own endpoint.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone,city,latitude,longitude
IMP-1,Alimentara Central,{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,Bucharest,44.4268,26.1025
IMP-2,"Smith, Jones & Co",{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,Cluj-Napoca,46.7712,23.6236

### Default mode is AllOrNothing: one bad row and nothing is written. Expect 200 with `imported: 0`
### — the import ran, and a file full of problems is its result rather than a malformed request.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone
IMP-GOOD,Would Have Worked,{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest
IMP-BAD,Bad Zone,{{channel.response.body.name}},Mars/Olympus_Mons

### Partial: the good rows land, and `rejectedRowsCsv` comes back in the shape it was sent, plus an
### `import_error` column. Fix that file, send that file — which is what stops a partial import from
### being a trap, since re-sending the original would now collide with everything that landed.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import?mode=Partial
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone,legacy_id
IMP-3,Landed Fine,{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,A-991
IMP-4,Bad Zone,{{channel.response.body.name}},Mars/Olympus_Mons,A-992

### A CSV has no types, so a Number custom field arrives as text and the catalogue is what says how
### to read it. Define `chiller_count` above first — this is the one thing the import does that
### POST /api/outlets does not, and it is parsing rather than a softer rule.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone,chiller_count
IMP-5,Typed From Text,{{channel.response.body.name}},Europe/Bucharest,3

### Expect 415 — JSON and Excel are the planned follow-ups, not yet read.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

[{"code":"IMP-6","name":"Not yet"}]

###

### ─── The territory covering an outlet (ORG-05, BR-OUT-1) ────────────────────────────────────────

### Outlets now carry their territory. Organization owns the answer — the membership rows are in its
### schema — so Outlets asks through `ITerritoryDirectory` rather than reading across (ADR-0005).
### One call for the whole page, which is why the contract takes a collection.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Put the outlet in a territory, then look again — `territory` fills in.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletIds": ["{{outlet.response.body.id}}"]
}

### Expect `territory` to name the one just assigned.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Take it out again. `territory` goes back to null — unassigned is an ordinary state, not an error:
### outlets are created before anyone decides who covers them.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/org/territories/{{territory.response.body.id}}/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

### ─── Paging, search and sort over the outlet base (OUT-01) ──────────────────────────────────────

### One page, with the total to draw a pager from. Offset rather than a cursor: a back office wants
### a total and the ability to jump; Sync keeps its own cursor feed for the job that needs one.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?page=1&pageSize=5
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Page 2 of the same list. Every row appears on exactly one page because the sort always ends on
### a unique column — without that tiebreak, equal sort keys have no defined order in SQL and a row
### can land on both pages while another lands on neither.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?page=2&pageSize=5&sort=Status
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Search matches code or name, ignoring case. Not channel or segment — that is what the filters
### are for, and folding them in would make "Modern" match every outlet in Modern Trade.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?search=corner
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A search containing a wildcard searches for that character. Unescaped, `%` matches everything
### while looking like a search that simply found a lot.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?search=50%25
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Sorted by channel — by its *name*, through a subquery. Ordering by the id would sort by GUID:
### stable, repeatable, and meaningless to whoever clicked the header.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?sort=Channel&descending=true&pageSize=5
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Filters and search narrow the total together — "1-50 of 812" has to be true of the filtered set,
### or the pager offers pages that are always empty.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?search=RO&status=Active&pageSize=5
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A page size nobody should ask for is clamped, not refused — the response echoes the size used,
### so a caller can see what happened without parsing an error. Expect `pageSize: 200`.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?pageSize=100000
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

### ─── What a refusal says about where it went wrong (api-contracts §3) ───────────────────────────

### One envelope for every refusal, whatever the status: `{ "errors": [{ "field", "message" }] }`.
### This one is a 400 and names `code` — the field the caller sent, not a column and not a form
### control, because the request is the only thing the API can promise anything about.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "",
  "name": "No code",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### The same shape at 409 — re-sending a code that already exists. A client reads both with one
### branch; before this, a 400 carried `{ errors: [...] }` and a 409 carried `{ error: "..." }`, so
### every caller had to sniff between two shapes to find out what went wrong.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0001",
  "name": "Duplicate code",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### Every problem at once, not the first. Six things are wrong here and six come back — someone
### filling a form wants to fix them in one pass, and reporting one at a time turns a six-field form
### into six round trips.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/iam/users
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "subjectId": "",
  "email": "",
  "displayName": "",
  "locale": "not-a-locale",
  "timeZone": "Mars/Olympus_Mons",
  "roleIds": []
}

### A custom field is named by the path it was sent under — `customFields.chiller_count`, not the
### bare key. The request has a `customFields` object, so that is where a client looks; the bare key
### would also collide with a fixed field the day a tenant defines one called `name`. Define
### `chiller_count` (0-50) above first.
###
### Expect 400 `outlet.customField.tooLarge`, with the bound in `args`. The same violation from the
### same shared rules is `product.customField.tooLarge` on `/api/products` — each module names its
### own codes (ADR-0012). Outlets' *other* refusals still carry prose only; migrating them is
### ADR-0012 stage 3.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0099",
  "name": "Too many chillers",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "customFields": { "chiller_count": 900 }
}

### `field: null` when the problem is about the request as a whole. A header row and nothing under it
### is not any one column's fault, and a form handed a field name for it would highlight a control at
### random — so it says so instead, and the screen shows it at the top.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone

### ─── The people at an outlet (OUT-01) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Contacts are personal data (B8) and are replaced wholesale, never patched — a delta needs the
### caller to know the current state, and two people editing one outlet would interleave silently.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0100",
  "name": "Contact Shop",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "contacts": [
    { "name": "Ana Ionescu", "role": "Buyer", "phone": "0721 555 111", "email": "ana@example.com" },
    { "name": "Bogdan Pop" }
  ]
}

### A contact with no name — expect 400 naming `contacts[0].name`. The name is the whole point of the
### record, since it is what a rep says at the counter; the rest is how to reach the person and any
### of it may not be known yet. Nothing refused this before, so it stored a row saying somebody
### exists without saying who.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0101",
  "name": "Nameless Contact",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "contacts": [{ "name": "   ", "role": "Buyer" }]
}

### The index is part of the answer — expect `contacts[1].email`. A form showing two people cannot
### work out from "not an email address" which of them it should highlight.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "code": "RO-BUC-0102",
  "name": "Phone In The Email Box",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "contacts": [
    { "name": "Ana Ionescu", "email": "ana@example.com" },
    { "name": "Bogdan Pop", "email": "0721 555 111" }
  ]
}

### Erasure: an empty list deletes every contact, and the rows are gone rather than flagged. This is
### the only deletion path today — the dedicated workflow is OUT-10.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "{{outlet.response.body.name}}",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest",
  "contacts": []
}

### Which is also why a client must send the list back on every save. Omitting `contacts` from a PUT
### is not "leave them alone" — it is the same as sending `[]`, and it is how a form that renders an
### outlet without its contacts quietly deletes them.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "name": "{{outlet.response.body.name}}",
  "channelId": "{{channel.response.body.id}}",
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Bucharest"
}


### What the import accepts, asked before a file is sent. The row cap lives here and nowhere else —
### a screen holding its own copy of 5,000 would drift silently, since nothing fails when the two
### disagree; the screen simply starts lying about the limit. `mediaTypes` widens on its own the day
### JSON and Excel land.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Held to outlet:write, like the import it describes. Expect 403 with a read-only token — a
### capability document is only about a capability, and someone with no import to configure is not
### owed the facts about one.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}


### A dry run hands the file back as it read it — `columns`, and `rows` of { row, values } aligned to
### them. That is what lets the import screen correct a row without parsing the upload a second time:
### two CSV readers only have to disagree about which row is row 7 for every flagged cell to land on
### the wrong shop, and nothing would say so. Note the quoted comma and the newline inside a field —
### the two cases a hand-rolled reader gets wrong, and why rows are counted as records, not lines.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import?dryRun=true
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone
RO-GRID-1,"Smith, Jones & Co",Modern Trade,Europe/Bucharest
RO-GRID-2,"Two
Lines Shop",Modren Trade,Europe/Bucharest

### A real run sends none of that back — nothing is left to correct, and the caller is holding the
### rows already. Expect `columns` and `rows` empty.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets/import
Content-Type: text/csv
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

code,name,channel,time_zone
RO-GRID-3,Corner Shop,Modern Trade,Europe/Bucharest

###

### ─── Call frequency (JRN-01) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### The default for a segment: A-grade shops are called on weekly. This is the rule an admin writes
### once instead of four thousand times, which is the whole reason a default exists.
###
### PUT keyed by the segment rather than POST, because a segment has at most one frequency — so
### saving twice has set it once. A second POST would have been a 409 about a row nobody asked to
### create.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments/A
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 1,
  "cycleLengthDays": 7
}

### Idempotent, and case-insensitive about which rule it means. "a" edits the rule "A" created
### rather than making a second one — two rules for one grade would leave resolution with two
### answers and a plan that changes between runs with nothing having been edited. The stored casing
### stays whatever created it.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments/a
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 2,
  "cycleLengthDays": 7
}

### What the tenant has graded.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### The exception: this particular shop is the chain's flagship and is worth four calls a month
### whatever its grade says. Without a per-outlet answer the only way to say this is to invent a
### segment for one shop, which corrupts the vocabulary every report groups by.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 4,
  "cycleLengthDays": 28
}

### Resolution — the ladder, and the answer generation actually consumes. Expect `source: "Outlet"`
### for the shop above, because the most specific rule that names it wins (the same shape BR-PRD-2
### resolves a price with: outlet → channel → default).
###
### `source` is returned rather than just the numbers because "why is this shop planned four times a
### month?" is the question an admin asks, and a number alone cannot answer it.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/resolve?outletId={{outlet.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Removing the exception falls back to the segment rather than to nothing — expect
### `source: "Segment"` from the same request above. That is the point of a ladder, and it is why
### this is a delete rather than "set it back to the default by hand".
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/outlets/{{outlet.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### An outlet nobody has graded, and nobody has overridden, is **absent** from the answer rather
### than present with a zero. A gap in configuration and a decision to never visit are different
### things, and only the first is something a screen should be able to list.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/resolve?outletId={{customOutlet.response.body.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Expect 400 `journey.frequency.visitsTooFew`. Zero is refused rather than stored as "never":
### a rule that plans nothing and no rule at all are the same outcome and different intentions, and
### the second is already expressible by not having a rule.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments/Z
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 0,
  "cycleLengthDays": 7
}

### Expect 400 `journey.frequency.cycleOutOfRange`, with the bound in `args`. A cycle longer than
### the planning horizon is a typo — 3650 for 365 — and it produces a rule that schedules nothing
### while looking configured.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments/Z
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 1,
  "cycleLengthDays": 3650
}

### Expect 400 `journey.frequency.unknownOutlet`. The outlet id is not a foreign key — Outlets is
### another module's schema (AT-1) — so this refusal is the only thing standing between a typo and a
### rule that resolves against nothing, forever, silently.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/outlets/019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-ffffffffffff
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 1,
  "cycleLengthDays": 7
}

### Held to journey:write. Expect 403 with the read-only token — setting how often a rep is sent
### somewhere is a sales-operations decision, and reading the plan is not the same as deciding it.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/frequencies/segments/A
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "visitsPerCycle": 1,
  "cycleLengthDays": 7
}

###

### ─── Working calendar (JRN-02) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### A rep's working pattern: which days they work, and how many calls fit in one.
###
### The days travel as **names**, never ordinals. `DayOfWeek`'s ordinals start the week on Sunday, so
### a plan built from numbers would be off by one in a way nobody reading the JSON would question.
###
### PUT keyed by the rep, like a segment frequency is keyed by its segment: a rep has at most one
### calendar, so setting it twice has set it once.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": ["Monday", "Wednesday", "Friday"],
  "visitsPerDay": 8
}

### Days are collapsed and sorted on the way in — a careless list is a caller being careless, not an
### admin describing something impossible. Expect `["Monday","Wednesday"]` back.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": ["Wednesday", "Monday", "Wednesday"],
  "visitsPerDay": 8
}

### Who has a pattern, and whose it is. `displayName` is null when the directory no longer resolves
### the subject — the calendar still stands, the same way an assignment to a removed user does.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A date nobody works. Tenant-wide: this is the exception to the pattern that everybody shares.
### Dated rather than recurring, because Easter moves and so do the substitute days a government
### grants when a fixed holiday falls on a weekend — a recurrence rule would be wrong every few
### years in a way nobody notices until a plan sends a rep out on a closed day.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/holidays
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "date": "2026-03-04",
  "name": "Company day"
}

### Expect 409 `journey.holiday.duplicate`. A tenant importing a year twice should end up with one
### Christmas, and the second import should say so rather than silently double it.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/holidays
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "date": "2026-03-04",
  "name": "Company day (again)"
}

### The pattern **minus** the holidays — the days generation can actually use, and the answer this
### slice exists to produce. Expect Monday 2 March and Friday 6 March, with the Wednesday gone.
###
### The closed day is *absent* rather than present with zero capacity: a day with no calls and a day
### nobody works are the same thing to a generator, and two shapes for one meaning is a filter every
### caller has to remember.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}/working-days?from=2026-03-02&to=2026-03-08
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Expect 400 `journey.calendar.noWorkingDays`. Same shape as a zero call frequency: a calendar with
### no days and no calendar at all produce the same empty plan, and the second already says it.
### Somebody on long-term leave is a deactivated user or an unassigned territory — both of which say
### so where the rest of the system can see them.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": [],
  "visitsPerDay": 8
}

### Expect 400 `journey.calendar.capacityOutOfRange`, with the bound in `args`. Nobody makes sixty
### calls in a day; a capacity that large is a typo that would let generation pack a whole cycle into
### one day while looking configured.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": ["Monday"],
  "visitsPerDay": 60
}

### Expect 400 `journey.calendar.unknownUser`. The rep is IAM's, and nothing about a subject id says
### which tenant it belongs to — without this a typo becomes a calendar no generation run will ever
### match to a person.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000deadbeef
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": ["Monday"],
  "visitsPerDay": 8
}

### Expect 400 `journey.calendar.periodTooLong`. This walks day by day, so an unbounded range is an
### unbounded loop reachable from an endpoint. A cycle is at most a year, so a year and a bit is the
### widest honest ask.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}/working-days?from=2026-01-01&to=2030-01-01
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A rep nobody has configured has no working days — **not** a 404. "Unconfigured" is the same
### answer frequency gives for an outlet nobody has graded, and it is not "no such rep": the caller
### asked about days, and a 404 would have answered a question about calendars instead.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000c0de/working-days?from=2026-03-02&to=2026-03-08
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Held to journey:write — expect 403 with the read-only token.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/calendars/{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "workingDays": ["Monday"],
  "visitsPerDay": 8
}

###

### ─── Journey plans (JRN-04) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Generate a plan. A POST that **writes**, not a GET that computes: a plan is the artefact a
### supervisor reviews, changes the inputs of, and regenerates — so each run is a thing with an id
### they can come back to and compare, rather than a number that vanishes with the tab.
###
### Four things must already be configured for this to contain anything: the rep covers a territory
### (ORG-04), that territory holds outlets, those outlets have a frequency (JRN-01), and the rep has
### a working calendar (JRN-02). Each missing one shows up differently in the response below.
# @name plan
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-02",
  "to": "2026-03-29"
}

### The response is deliberately more than the visits. `shortfalls` names outlets the calendar could
### not fit as often as their frequency asks — the sentence a supervisor needs, because a plan that
### is 25% short otherwise looks exactly like a complete one. `excluded` names the shops that were
### skipped outright and why: `Closed` (BR-JRN-5) or `NoFrequency` (nobody has graded it).
###
### Note which of the two is **stored**: shortfalls are, exclusions are not. A shortfall is a
### statement about *this* plan against the capacity it had, and nothing else records it; an
### exclusion is recoverable at any time from Outlets or the frequency screen.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Publishing is a separate act, and that separation is the point. Until it happens the plan is an
### experiment — generation is cheap and repeatable, so a supervisor runs it, changes a frequency and
### runs it again, and none of those should be something a device is downloading.
###
### This is also what raises `JourneyPublished` to the outbox. Sync is the consumer and does not
### exist yet (W8); an event is true whether or not anyone is listening, which is the same shape
### `PriceListPublished` has had since W6.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/publish
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Expect 409 `journey.plan.alreadyPublished`. Refused rather than treated as a no-op: a second
### publish is either a double-click or somebody expecting it to re-announce a changed plan, and the
### second is a misunderstanding worth correcting. A published plan does not change — the rep may
### already have walked half of it, and rewriting the rows underneath them would discard the visits
### they have completed. Replanning produces a *new* plan.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/publish
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### What this rep has been given, newest window first.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans?userId={{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Expect 400 `journey.plan.unknownUser`. Without it, a mistyped subject produces an *empty* plan —
### which reads as "this rep covers nothing", a coverage problem rather than a typo, and the two are
### fixed in completely different places.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000deadbeef",
  "from": "2026-03-02",
  "to": "2026-03-29"
}

### Expect 400 `journey.plan.windowBackwards`. Refused rather than quietly producing an empty plan,
### for the same reason as above: an empty plan says something about the rep, not about the request.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-29",
  "to": "2026-03-02"
}

### Expect 400 `journey.plan.windowTooLong`, with the bound in `args`. Generation walks the window
### day by day, so an unbounded range is an unbounded loop reachable from a request body.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-01-01",
  "to": "2030-01-01"
}

### Held to journey:write — expect 403. Generating a plan decides what a rep will be sent to do, so
### it is a sales-operations act rather than a reading one.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "userId": "{{newUser.response.body.subjectId}}",
  "from": "2026-03-02",
  "to": "2026-03-29"
}

###

### ─── What a rep may do to their round (JRN-06) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Held to **journey:annotate**, not journey:write. A rep reports on the round they walked; they do
### not decide what the round is. Folding the two together to let somebody record a closed shop would
### also let them generate and publish plans for anyone — the difference between a permission model
### and a tier list.

### A call that did not happen, and why. This is where VIS-07 lives: capturing the reason against the
### *planned* call means the Visit module never grows a state for a visit that did not happen — no
### half-created visit with no check-in, no outcome, and a reason field that is null for every real
### one.
###
### The reason is required. A skipped shop with no explanation is a gap nobody can act on, and
### BR-JRN-6 would inherit the blank.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits/{{plan.response.body.visits.0.id}}/not-visited
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "reason": "Shutters down, no answer"
}

### The call stays on the plan — it does not disappear. **BR-JRN-2**, and the absence of a DELETE
### route is the enforcement: a shop that was skipped is a fact about the round, and letting a rep
### remove it would make coverage look complete and turn BR-JRN-6 into a measure of what was left on
### the plan rather than of what was done.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Expect 409 `journey.visit.alreadyNotVisited`. The first reason is what the rep saw on the day;
### silently replacing it would leave two different answers to "why was this shop missed".
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits/{{plan.response.body.visits.0.id}}/not-visited
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "reason": "Changed my mind"
}

### Moving a call **within its cycle** — a rep organising their own week. No event: nothing outside
### this module changes, because the outlet still gets its call in the cycle its frequency asked for.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits/{{plan.response.body.visits.1.id}}/reschedule
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "date": "2026-03-05"
}

### Expect 400 `journey.visit.outsideCycle` — **BR-JRN-4**. A week later is the next cycle: the shop
### would be covered twice in one and not at all in the other, which changes compliance for both.
### That is a supervisor's decision, not a rep's.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits/{{plan.response.body.visits.1.id}}/reschedule
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "date": "2026-03-16"
}

### A call nobody planned — a shop the rep passed, or one that asked them in. It joins the plan as
### `Unplanned`, which is what keeps "did the plan work?" answerable separately from "what did the
### rep actually do?".
###
### It belongs to no cycle, so it cannot be rescheduled: BR-JRN-4 is about moving a call within the
### cycle its *frequency* put it in, and this one was never in one. A rep who wants it on another day
### adds it on that day.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "date": "2026-03-04"
}

### Expect 400 `journey.visit.unknownOutlet`. A rep adding a shop this tenant does not have is a
### client bug, and storing it would put an unresolvable id on a plan that reporting later has to
### explain.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-ffffffffffff",
  "date": "2026-03-04"
}

### Expect 403 with the read-only token: it holds journey:read and neither journey:write nor
### journey:annotate. Reading a plan and reporting on one are different acts.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/journey/plans/{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}/visits/{{plan.response.body.visits.0.id}}/not-visited
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "reason": "Closed"
}

###

### ─── Visit workflow, per channel (VIS-03) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### How a visit is worked in one channel: the ordered steps, and whether the rep is expected to be
### there. Configuration owns what a tenant may flex; Visit owns what happens when a rep works, and
### this is the seam between them.
###
### Built one slice *ahead* of its consumer on purpose — BR-VIS-2's override rule cannot be written
### without somewhere to ask whether presence was expected, so check-in depends on this rather than
### the other way round.
###
### `order` is not sent. The position in the list is the order: a client that supplies its own
### numbers can send 1, 2, 2, 7, and then every consumer has to decide what a gap or a tie means.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Audit", "mandatory": true, "label": "Shelf check" },
    { "type": "Order", "mandatory": false, "label": "Take an order" },
    { "type": "Note", "mandatory": false, "label": "Anything else" }
  ]
}

### `mandatory` is a property of the *step*, not of its type — BR-VIS-3 gates check-out on it, and
### the same kind of work is required in one channel and optional in another: an audit is the job in
### modern trade and a courtesy in a kiosk.

### A channel nobody has configured **still answers** — no steps, and presence expected. That is the
### contract's "never null", and the direction matters: presence expected on a remote channel records
### an exception for every ordinary call (annoying, and visible), while presence *not* expected on a
### store channel silently stops recording the one thing BR-VIS-2 exists to capture.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-ffffffffffff
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A remote-capable channel. A phone call, a video call or a head-office meeting is legitimately not
### at the outlet, so demanding an override reason would record an exception where nothing
### exceptional happened — and a flag that fires on ordinary work is one supervisors learn to ignore.
###
### This also replaces the sequence above wholesale rather than patching it: a workflow is an
### *ordered* thing, so a patch would need the caller to know the current order to say anything about
### it, and two admins editing one channel would interleave into a sequence neither designed.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": false,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Order", "mandatory": true, "label": "Take the order" }
  ]
}

### An empty step list is allowed and is not a mistake: check in, check out — a presence call is a
### real thing, and refusing it would force an admin to invent a step to describe the simplest
### possible visit.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": []
}

### Expect 400 `config.workflow.labelRequired`, and note the **indexed** field — `steps[1].label`.
### An admin looking at eight steps cannot work out which one "a step needs a label" is about.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Audit", "mandatory": true, "label": "Shelf check" },
    { "type": "Note", "mandatory": false, "label": "  " }
  ]
}

### Expect 400 `config.workflow.unknownChannel`. A workflow keyed to a channel this tenant does not
### have is one no visit will ever resolve — it would sit in the list looking configured.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-fffffffffffe
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": []
}

### Expect 400, not 500 — the step type travels by name, so a name that is not one of the names is
### the caller's mistake. Same rule as every other enum on this API (api-contracts §3.1).
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Interpretive dance", "mandatory": false, "label": "x" }
  ]
}

### Deleting returns the channel to the default rather than leaving visits in it unworkable. There is
### no "no workflow" state a visit has to handle, which is exactly what makes deleting one safe.
DELETE {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Held to config:write, like every other thing a tenant configures — expect 403.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": []
}

###

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit — check-in (VIS-01, VIS-02, BR-VIS-2), W7 slice 7
#
# The rep is never in the body. Every request below is "whoever this token is, at this outlet" —
# a field the caller could fill in is a field they could fill in wrong, and the visit is the
# record of who was where.
#
# `outlet` above is at 44.4638, 26.0946 with a 150 m default radius, so the two positions used
# here are the doorway and a point ~2.2 km north of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Standing in the shop. Expect 201, `wasInsideGeofence: true`, no override reason.
# @name visit
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### Somewhere else entirely. Expect 400 `visit.checkIn.overrideReasonRequired` — and read the
### `args`: the refusal carries the distance and the radius, because "eighty metres" and "two
### kilometres" are different conversations and a rep deserves to see which one this is.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4838,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### The same position with a sentence attached. Expect 201 — BR-VIS-2 asks, it does not refuse.
### The distance is kept alongside the reason so a supervisor reviewing it sees both.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4838,
  "longitude": 26.0946,
  "overrideReason": "Owner asked me to meet him at the depot"
}

### A device with no fix at a shop that has one. Expect 400 — nothing can be measured, and that is
### precisely why it needs saying: this is also how a check-in would be faked.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}"
}

### Half a position — expect 400 `visit.checkIn.halfPosition`. A latitude on its own is not a place.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638
}

### Not on the earth — expect 400 `visit.checkIn.latitudeOutOfRange`, not a stored impossibility.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 91,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### An outlet this tenant does not have — expect 400 `visit.checkIn.unknownOutlet`. Another
### tenant's outlet answers identically; an existence check that did not would leak the id.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-fffffffffffe",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### The visit as it was recorded — position, distance, verdict and reason, not just the verdict.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{visit.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### A rep's own day, or a supervisor looking at one shop. Held to visit:read.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits?outletId={{outlet.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Reading is visit:read — the viewer has it, so expect 200.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Checking in is visit:write, which the viewer does not have — expect 403. This is the first
### write in the system a field rep holds and a back-office viewer does not.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

###

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit — steps (VIS-03, VIS-04, BR-VIS-3), W7 slice 8
#
# A visit's steps are a **copy** of the channel's workflow, taken at check-in. Configure the
# workflow first, then check in, and note that editing the workflow afterwards changes nothing
# about the visit already running — that is BR-VIS-6's snapshot rule applied to the one piece of
# reference data that decides whether a visit can end.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Give the channel a workflow: two mandatory steps and one optional note.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Audit", "mandatory": true, "label": "Shelf check" },
    { "type": "Order", "mandatory": true, "label": "Take the order" },
    { "type": "Note", "mandatory": false, "label": "Anything else" }
  ]
}

### Check in. The response now carries `steps` and `openMandatorySteps` — the rep sees what stands
### between them and the door from the first screen, not at check-out.
# @name workedVisit
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### Do the first one. Expect 200 and the whole visit back, with `openMandatorySteps` one shorter.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{workedVisit.response.body.steps.0.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### Doing it twice — expect 409 `visit.step.alreadyCompleted`. The first completion's timestamp is
### a fact about the rep's day; restamping it would make time-on-step a measure of the last edit.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{workedVisit.response.body.steps.0.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### A note step is its text. Ticked with nothing written — expect 400 `visit.step.noteRequired`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{workedVisit.response.body.steps.2.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "notes": "   "
}

### The same step with something in it — expect 200, and the text trimmed.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{workedVisit.response.body.steps.2.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "notes": "  Competitor ran a 2+1 on the top shelf.  "
}

### A step id that is not on this visit — expect 404. The step id alone does not name a step; it
### has to be on the visit the route named, or one rep could tick another's work knowing a guid.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-fffffffffffe/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### Working a step is visit:write, same as checking in — doing the work and saying you did it are
### the same act. Expect 403.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{workedVisit.response.body.steps.1.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### Now rewrite the channel's workflow entirely...
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Survey", "mandatory": true, "label": "New mandatory survey" }
  ]
}

### ...and read the visit back: the steps are the ones it started with. A rep who checked in at ten
### is not refused check-out at four for a step that did not exist when they walked in.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{workedVisit.response.body.visit.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit — check-out and seal (VIS-05, BR-VIS-3/4/5), W7 slice 9
#
# This is the end of the visit that says no. BR-VIS-2 refuses to keep a rep out of a shop;
# BR-VIS-3 refuses to file a visit as done while the work it was configured for is not — and
# nothing is lost by refusing, because the rep is still in the shop and the refusal names the
# steps. After it, BR-VIS-4: the visit is sealed and every write path refuses.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### A visit with one mandatory step, to be refused and then finished.
PUT {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/config/visit-workflows/{{channel.response.body.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "presenceExpected": true,
  "steps": [
    { "type": "Audit", "mandatory": true, "label": "Shelf check" },
    { "type": "Note", "mandatory": false, "label": "Anything else" }
  ]
}

### Check in, so there is a visit to end.
# @name endingVisit
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

### Expect 400 `visit.checkOut.mandatoryStepsOpen` — and read `args.steps`: the refusal lists what
### is outstanding by label, because "not yet" is what sends a rep back into the shop twice.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "Productive"
}

### Do the mandatory one.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{endingVisit.response.body.steps.0.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{}

### Non-productive with no reason — expect 400 `visit.checkOut.reasonRequired`. "Why was nothing
### sold" is the reporting fact; without it a non-productive call is a row nobody can act on.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "NonProductive"
}

### Now check out. The optional note step is still pending and that is fine — BR-VIS-3 is about
### mandatory work. Expect 200 with `timeOnSiteSeconds`, and the check-out position recorded but
### **not judged**: there is no geofence rule at this end (VIS-05).
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "NonProductive",
  "reason": "Owner away until Thursday, no order taken",
  "latitude": 44.4840,
  "longitude": 26.0950
}

### Sealed (BR-VIS-4). A second check-out — expect 409 `visit.checkOut.alreadyCheckedOut`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "Productive"
}

### And a late step completion — the queued write a device pushes after the visit has gone.
### Expect 409 `visit.step.visitSealed`, not a quiet success.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/steps/{{endingVisit.response.body.steps.1.id}}/complete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "notes": "Something I forgot"
}

### An outcome that is not one of the outcomes — expect 400, not 500 (api-contracts §3.1).
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "Splendid"
}

### Held to visit:write, like every other thing a rep records — expect 403.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}/check-out
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{viewerToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outcome": "Productive"
}

### The sealed visit as it stands: two positions, an outcome, a reason and a duration.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/{{endingVisit.response.body.visit.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit — claiming a planned call (JRN-04, W7 slice 9b)
#
# A visit may say which planned call it fulfils. Until slice 9b that id was taken on trust, and a
# fabricated one would have surfaced first as a coverage report showing a call that was made.
# Journey now answers the whole question — this rep's, at this shop, on a published plan — and
# every kind of miss gets the same refusal, so this route cannot be used to find out what is on
# somebody else's round.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### A planned call nobody planned. Expect 400 `visit.checkIn.unknownPlannedCall`.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946,
  "plannedVisitId": "019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-fffffffffffe"
}

### Another rep's call, a call at the wrong shop, and a call on an unpublished plan all answer
### exactly the same way — deliberately. Substitute a real planned visit id from
### `GET /api/journey/plans/{id}` above to see it.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946,
  "plannedVisitId": "{{plan.response.body.plan.id}}"
}

### No planned call at all is ordinary (JRN-06) — a shop the rep walked past. Expect 201.
POST {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/visits/check-in
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

{
  "outletId": "{{outlet.response.body.id}}",
  "latitude": 44.4638,
  "longitude": 26.0946
}

###

### Name a set of shops in one request, rather than one GET per id (W7 slice 10c). The journey plan
### screen asks this: a plan is hundreds of visits over a few dozen shops.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?ids={{outlet.response.body.id}},{{customOutlet.response.body.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### An id this tenant does not have is simply absent — not a 404, and nothing about shops the caller
### may not see. Expect one item back, not two.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?ids={{outlet.response.body.id}},019fffff-ffff-7fff-bfff-fffffffffffe
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Asking about **no** shops answers about no shops. `ids=` is not the same as omitting `ids`,
### which is why the client sends it: an empty list means "none of them".
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?ids=
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

### Not an id at all — expect 400 `outlet.list.idMalformed`. Skipping it would answer with a shorter
### list than was asked for, and the caller's only clue would be a shop that quietly has no name.
GET {{Server_HostAddress}}/api/outlets?ids={{outlet.response.body.id}},not-a-guid
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken.response.body.access_token}}

###

### ── Sync · device registry (OFF-12) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Binding needs a token but no permission: a rep registers their own phone as part of
# signing in on it. The device belongs to the subject in the token — note there is no
# user id in the body, deliberately, so one cannot be supplied.

POST {{host}}/api/sync/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Maria's Pixel 8" }

### Bind a second device — this deactivates the first as `Swapped`, which keeps its right
### to one final drain-push (A8). One active device per rep, enforced by a unique index.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Maria's replacement" }

### The rep's own devices, active first. No permission — seeing which of your own phones
### is bound is not oversight of anybody.
GET {{host}}/api/sync/devices/mine
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}

### Everyone's devices — the administrator's view, and the one that needs device:read.
GET {{host}}/api/sync/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Revoke a lost or stolen device. `Compromised` blocks the final drain-push that
### `Swapped` allows: a suspect device must not push fabricated visits (security §5).
### The reason is required rather than defaulted — a default would pick a security
### posture on the administrator's behalf.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/devices/{{deviceId}}/revoke
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "Compromised" }

### ── Sync · pull (OFF-03) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reference delta. Cursor 0 (or omitted) means "I have nothing" — a first sync or a
# rebind — and the server answers with everything in the rep's territory.
#
# The device check runs before scope is resolved: an unknown or revoked device is refused
# without the server reading Organization's assignments, so a revoked phone learns nothing
# from how long the refusal takes.
#
# One cursor per entity type, because they advance independently — a tenant that edits
# outlets hourly and publishes a plan monthly would, on a shared cursor, make every outlet
# edit look like a journey change. It is also what makes adding an entity type free: a
# device that has never sent `journeys` gets its whole round and keeps its outlet watermark.
#
# Note what each is scoped by. Outlets: the rep's *territory*, as of today. Journeys: the
# rep named on the *plan* — a call at a shop that has since left their territory is still
# their call, and scoping the round by outlet would hide exactly the one a supervisor would
# ask about.

POST {{host}}/api/sync/pull
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}", "cursors": { "outlets": 0, "journeys": 0 } }

### A second pull at the cursor the first returned. Carries only what changed since —
### an unchanged tenant answers with an empty page and the same cursor.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/pull
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}", "cursors": { "outlets": 42, "journeys": 17 } }

### A device that was replaced answers 409 `sync.pull.deviceInactive` — the client's job is
### to bind again, which re-snapshots from zero.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/pull
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "deviceId": "{{revokedDeviceId}}", "cursors": { "outlets": 0, "journeys": 0 } }

### ── Sync · push (OFF-04) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The drain. Every mutation carries an id minted on the device, and the ledger answers a
# replay with what happened the first time rather than applying it again — the device
# cannot tell a lost response from a lost request, so it retries, and the retry is free.
#
# Note what the visit carries: the device's own timestamps and its own geofence verdict.
# The server stores both unmodified. Re-judging yesterday's visit against today's radius
# would reclassify a rep who was legitimately inside it.

POST {{host}}/api/sync/push
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}",
  "mutations": [
    {
      "mutationId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000a001",
      "type": "CapturedVisit",
      "visit": {
        "visitId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000b001",
        "outletId": "{{outletId}}",
        "plannedVisitId": null,
        "checkedInAtUtc": "2026-03-17T09:00:00+00:00",
        "checkInLatitude": 44.4325,
        "checkInLongitude": 26.1039,
        "checkInDistanceMetres": 12.5,
        "wasInsideGeofence": true,
        "overrideReason": null,
        "steps": [
          {
            "stepId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000c001",
            "order": 1,
            "type": "Note",
            "mandatory": true,
            "label": "Talk to the owner",
            "notes": "Reordered two cases.",
            "completedAtUtc": "2026-03-17T09:10:00+00:00"
          }
        ],
        "outcome": "Productive",
        "outcomeReason": null,
        "checkedOutAtUtc": "2026-03-17T09:25:00+00:00",
        "checkOutLatitude": 44.4325,
        "checkOutLongitude": 26.1039
      }
    }
  ]
}

### The same request again, byte for byte — what a device sends when the first response
### never arrived. Answers `accepted` a second time and stores nothing: one visit exists.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/push
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}",
  "mutations": [
    {
      "mutationId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000a001",
      "type": "CapturedVisit",
      "visit": {
        "visitId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000b001",
        "outletId": "{{outletId}}",
        "plannedVisitId": null,
        "checkedInAtUtc": "2026-03-17T09:00:00+00:00",
        "checkInLatitude": 44.4325,
        "checkInLongitude": 26.1039,
        "checkInDistanceMetres": 12.5,
        "wasInsideGeofence": true,
        "overrideReason": null,
        "steps": [],
        "outcome": "Productive",
        "outcomeReason": null,
        "checkedOutAtUtc": "2026-03-17T09:25:00+00:00",
        "checkOutLatitude": 44.4325,
        "checkOutLongitude": 26.1039
      }
    }
  ]
}

### Partial success, which is the normal case rather than an error path. The first visit
### lands; the second is refused `visit.ingest.outcomeReasonRequired` (BR-VIS-3) and the
### response is 200 with per-mutation results, because failing the batch would lose the one
### that was fine. A rejection is recorded too — otherwise the device retries it forever.
POST {{host}}/api/sync/push
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}",
  "mutations": [
    {
      "mutationId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000a002",
      "type": "CapturedVisit",
      "visit": {
        "visitId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000b002",
        "outletId": "{{outletId}}",
        "plannedVisitId": null,
        "checkedInAtUtc": "2026-03-17T11:00:00+00:00",
        "checkInLatitude": null,
        "checkInLongitude": null,
        "checkInDistanceMetres": null,
        "wasInsideGeofence": false,
        "overrideReason": "No signal, shop is in a basement.",
        "steps": [],
        "outcome": "Productive",
        "outcomeReason": null,
        "checkedOutAtUtc": "2026-03-17T11:20:00+00:00",
        "checkOutLatitude": null,
        "checkOutLongitude": null
      }
    },
    {
      "mutationId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000a003",
      "type": "CapturedVisit",
      "visit": {
        "visitId": "0195e7c4-0000-7000-8000-00000000b003",
        "outletId": "{{outletId}}",
        "plannedVisitId": null,
        "checkedInAtUtc": "2026-03-17T13:00:00+00:00",
        "checkInLatitude": null,
        "checkInLongitude": null,
        "checkInDistanceMetres": null,
        "wasInsideGeofence": true,
        "overrideReason": null,
        "steps": [],
        "outcome": "NonProductive",
        "outcomeReason": null,
        "checkedOutAtUtc": "2026-03-17T13:05:00+00:00",
        "checkOutLatitude": null,
        "checkOutLongitude": null
      }
    }
  ]
}

### A device reported `Compromised` answers 403 `sync.push.deviceCompromised`. This is the
### one revocation that closes the drain: a `Swapped` phone may still send the work it
### captured, a suspect one may not send anything (security §5).
POST {{host}}/api/sync/push
Authorization: Bearer {{repToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "deviceId": "{{compromisedDeviceId}}", "mutations": [] }
